{"id": "66449713-cb7a-4d39-8380-33ba60de9fe9", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:21:06.345181", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "a9ee41b4-4e62-476e-b963-501b06e2f064", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: repo is empty except task.json. No ML dependencies, no network, no datasets, no GPU.\n\nNow the design. The researcher topics reference \"neurips2024/94328/2:setup_*\" — I can't access these. This likely refers to a paper on KV cache quantization / activation quantization with a clipping ratio at runtime. Possibly \"KV Cache is 128 Bit per Token\", or the paper could be about \"runtime quantization with clipping ratio\". I should note that I cannot access topic docs and flag as an assumption/uncertainty — the researcher may need to supply setup details (bit-widths, eval set, sequence length).\n\nNow write a proposed plan (stage=plan) covering:\n- Repository inspection findings.\n- Proposed design:\n  - Model: Llama-2-7B (meta-llama/Llama-2-7B-hf) — access restricted; flag as blocker; alternative Llama-3? No, must be LLAMA2-7B per fixed requirements. Note tokenizer/config replication.\n  - Quantization scheme: symmetric uniform (affine-free) quantization for inputs (activations: input embeddings? \"input quantization\" — likely input activations to linear layers, i.e., per-token per-group symmetric), asymmetric (zero-point min/max) for KV cache.\n  - Clipping ratio alpha: scale = alpha * max(|x|) for symmetric; for asymmetric, clip the [min, max] range toward the mean by alpha, i.e., clip bounds as mean ± alpha*half-range, or clip percentile.\n  - Sweep grid: alpha ∈ {1.00 (hard clip to minmax/no clip), 0.99, 0.98, 0.97, 0.95, 0.92, 0.90, 0.85, 0.80} — a coarse-to-fine grid. Plus alternative estimators: MAD-based sigma*k (k ∈ {3,3.5,4,4.5,5}) and percentile-based (99.9, 99.99, 100 - equivalent to max). Map MAD/percentile to an \"equivalent alpha\" for unified PPL-vs-ratio curves.\n  - Decoupled sweeps first: fix alpha_KV=1.0 sweep alpha_in, then fix alpha_in=1.0 sweep alpha_KV; then a small joint confirmation grid near the optima (e.g., 3x3 or 5x5 cross product around best) to check interaction/non-additivity; optionally a few diagonal points.\n  - Bit-width: not pinned → treat as a factor with primary condition W8A8 for inputs / KV 4-bit asymmetric (matching typical NeurIPS-2024 KV quantization setups, e.g., KIVI-style 2-bit? ) Hmm. Must not silently choose. Propose primary grid at (input activations 8-bit symmetric per-token; KV 4-bit asymmetric per-channel K / per-token V? ) but flag and request researcher confirmation via topics. Provide sensitivity: replicate the alpha sweep at KV ∈ {2,3,4,8} bit and input ∈ {4,8} bit at a coarse alpha grid to test whether optimal alpha shifts with bit-width — key point: optimal alpha is bit-width dependent, so the \"optimal fixed ratio\" answer requires bit-width specification.\n  - Eval set: WikiText-2 test split, contiguous 2048-token sequences (non-overlapping), sequence-level PPL = exp(mean next-token NLL with padded IGNORE_INDEX for tokens not predicted); also C4 validation subset (128 or 256 sequences of 2048) as a second distribution. Also long-context 4096 check for KV.\n  - Controls: FP16 baseline (PPL reference), alpha=1.0 exact min/max (\"no-clip\" in the sense of full range coverage, i.e., no outlier truncation), and hard-clip extremes alpha → 0.70/0.60 or MSE-optimal clip and \"perfect\" oracle clip per-tensor minimizing MSE; also fake-quant identity check (quantize-dequantize at 16-bit math to isolate clipping effect from kernel error), and per-group vs per-token/per-channel granularity as a confounder control.\n  - Analysis: PPL vs alpha curves per factor; ΔPPL relative to FP16 and to alpha=1.0; sensitivity attribution: fit ΔPPL_total ≈ f_in(alpha_in) + f_KV(alpha_KV) and test additivity with the joint grid; report argmin alpha* and curvature; optimal defined as argmin PPL with tie-breaks: within noise band (bootstrap CI over sequences), prefer larger alpha (less clipping, closer to min/max, simpler / less MSE for outliers?) Actually tie-break: choose alpha within 0.5% of best PPL that has the largest alpha (least aggressive clipping → lower risk on other tasks / better generalization); also require stability across WikiText-2 and C4 and across seeds (deterministic, so across eval subsets / sequence orderings). Report statistical significance via paired per-sequence NLL differences (paired bootstrap / t-test on per-sequence log-loss).\n  - Feasibility: no GPU, no deps, no network → cannot run 7B inference; 224 CPUs and 2TB RAM, but torch/transformers not installed and pip network disabled. So execution requires provisioning: offline wheels, model weights. Propose staged plan: Stage A smoke test on a tiny Llama-architecture model (Llama-3.2-1B-equivalent? Or random-init 2-layer Llama config) to validate the quantization hooks and PPL harness; Stage B scaled-down real-model runs (e.g., 128 sequences × 2048) on CPU with fp16 on CPU, batch 1, ~ expected hours per config; estimate cost. Note that on CPU, one Llama-7B PPL eval of 128×2048 tokens ≈ maybe 1-3 hours with 224 cores; k quantized configs × alpha grid could be large → prune grid, use per-sequence early stopping / subsample, reuse FP16 NLL baseline.\n  - Important: fake-quant (dequantize to fp16) means the compute cost is identical regardless of alpha → no memory savings measured; the design measures PPL only, which is the required metric. Optionally record theoretical memory and clipping-error metrics (NMSE, outlier fraction) as cheap diagnostics computable from a few captured activation/KV tensors — cheap proxy to prune the grid (cheap surrogate: capture activation/KV tensors from a single FP16 pass over eval set... storing all is too much; capture from a subset of layers/sequences).\n  \n  - Also mention the \"runtime\" aspect: dynamic per-token/per-channel clipping computed at inference time from actual tensor statistics with fixed alpha multiplier, vs static offline-calibrated clip (MSE-optimal). Design distinguishes \"runtime dynamic scaled-max (alpha)\" as primary; static-calibrated as a comparison.\n  - Also: attention sink / initial-token handling in KV quantization (K per-channel, V per-token; keep first residual window in fp16) — flag as a design choice affecting optimal alpha for KV.\n\nOpen uncertainties: bit-width (KV and input), quantization granularity (per-token/per-channel/per-group size 32/64/128), which \"input\" (embedding inputs vs all activation inputs vs weight-input?), KIVI-style 2-bit, sequence length 2048 vs 4096, WikiText-2 vs C4 primary, whether alpha is applied to absmax vs percentile, availability of the model weights, whether the researcher's referenced topics specify details (I could not read the topic docs).\n\nAlso I should ask clarifying questions (question_budget 6, but I use end_researcher_turn not a question tool — I'll list questions in the plan).\n\nLet me check python availability of anything else like a package index / offline wheels, to make feasibility claims accurate.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "pip download numpy -d /tmp/x --no-deps 2>&1 | tail -3; echo '---net---'; timeout 5 python3 -c \"import urllib.request;print(urllib.request.urlopen('https://pypi.org/simple/',timeout=4).status)\" 2>&1|tail -2; echo '---'; ls /usr/lib/python3/dist-packages | head -30; echo '---'; gcc --version 2>&1|head -1", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a00706efa90c1963", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-a00706efa90c1963", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"pip download numpy -d /tmp/x --no-deps 2>&1 | tail -3; echo '---net---'; timeout 5 python3 -c \\\"import urllib.request;print(urllib.request.urlopen('https://pypi.org/simple/',timeout=4).status)\\\" 2>&1|tail -2; echo '---'; ls /usr/lib/python3/dist-packages | head -30; echo '--