{"id": "6b42aef0-78b2-422b-9749-9ce65c419b13", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:23:26.920866", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "7dbdd58d-5c32-4d1d-a7b9-02b498d6b2cd", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Repository and environment inspected. Key facts before proposing the design:\n\n- `/workspace` contains **only `task.json`** — no quantization code, no clipping/ratio logic, no LLAMA-2 hooks, no datasets (no WikiText-2/C4), no eval scripts. Effectively an empty repo.\n- Environment: Python 3.14.4, **no `numpy`, no `torch`, no `transformers`, no `datasets`, no `sentencepiece`**; `pip download` fails and DNS resolution fails → **no network, no way to fetch dependencies or Llama-2-7B weights**.\n- Hardware: 224 CPUs, ~2 TB RAM, no GPU, ~1 TB tmpfs scratch. gcc 15 available.\n- `task.json` states: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n\nI could not read the `neurips2024/94328/2:setup_*` topic documents from the sandbox, so setup specifics must come from you."}], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: no network, no numpy, no torch. Design-only.\n\nNow write the plan via end_researcher_turn stage=plan. Keep it structured but rich enough. Then wait for approval.\n\nI'll write a thorough plan in the answer.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## A. What is actually in /workspace (verified)\n\n- `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` → only `/workspace/task.json`. **No quantization implementation, no clipping/ratio code, no Llama hooks/monkey-patches, no datasets, no perplexity eval scripts, no configs, no git history.** The repo is empty apart from the task statement.\n- `task.json` mirrors the public question verbatim and adds `\"track.md\": \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"`\n- Toolchain: `python3` = 3.14.4; `import torch` and `import numpy` both `ModuleNotFoundError`; `pip list` shows only OS/Ansible/apt-era packages (no numpy, scipy, torch, transformers, datasets, tokenizers, sentencepiece, accelerate).\n- No network: DNS resolution fails (`Temporary failure in name resolution`), `pip download numpy` fails. So neither dependencies nor `meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf` weights nor WikiText-2/C4 can be obtained in-sandbox.\n- Compute: `nproc` = 224, RAM ≈ 2011 GB, swap 0, `/tmp` tmpfs ≈ 1 TB, gcc 15.2 present. No GPU.\n- No local copy of the `neurips2024/94328/2:setup_{1,10,17}` topic docs was found (searched `/` for `*neurips*`, `*94328*`), so any source-specific setup details must come from you.\n\nConclusion: this is a from-scratch design; nothing can be executed here, consistent with the design-only framing.\n\n## B. Proposed experiment design (plan, not yet full design doc)\n\n**Fixed by the task (non-negotiable):** model = LLAMA2-7B; KV-cache quantization is **asymmetric** (zero-point/min-max form); input quantization is **symmetric** (absmax, no zero point); metric = perplexity; the manipulated variable is the **runtime clipping ratio**.\n\n**1. Rationale / hypothesis.** Runtime (dynamic, per-tensor-group) clipping rescales the quantization grid below the observed extremum: too large (α→1) wastes code on outliers → large uniform-step error; too small (α≪1) truncates outliers → bias error. PPL should therefore be U-shaped (usually right-skewed, near-flat then sharp degradation) in α, with the optimum shifted *lower* as bit-width decreases and *more* pronounced for asymmetric KV quantization, because asymmetric min/max is more outlier-sensitive than symmetric absmax. Activation outliers (massive-activation layers) and attention-sink tokens in K are expected to dominate sensitivity, which the design isolates via decoupled sweeps.\n\n**2. Quantizer definitions (one code path, fake-quant/dequantize in fp so PPL is the only moving part).**\n- Inputs (embedding/activation inputs to quantized ops): symmetric uniform, scale s = α · max|x| / (2^(b−1) − 1), round-to-nearest, group granularity per-token × group_size g (default g = group-along-channels; g flagged as uncertainty).\n- KV cache: asymmetric uniform with clipped bounds [ℓ, u] shrunk by α toward the group center: ℓ′ = c − α(c − ℓ), u′ = c + α(u − c), c = (ℓ+u)/2; K quantized per-channel, V per-token (both flagged; keep first `RES=128` attention-sink tokens/positions in fp16 — flagged).\n- α applied as a **multiplicative clip ratio on the runtime statistic** (primary variant, \"scaled-max/scaled-range\"). Two alternative ratio families to test as robustness: **MAD-based** (α↔σ·k, k∈{3,3.5,4,4.5,5,∞}) and **percentile-based** (q∈{99.99, 99.9, 99.5, 99.0, 95.0}) — mapped onto the α axis by the equivalent clipping mass so curves are comparable.\n\n**3. Sweep structure — decoupled first (as you requested), then a small joint block.**\n- Phase 1 (KV frozen at α_KV = 1.0): α_in ∈ {1.00, 0.99, 0.98, 0.96, 0.94, 0.92, 0.90, 0.85, 0.80} → 9 runs.\n- Phase 2 (input frozen at α_in = 1.0): α_KV ∈ same 9-point grid → 9 runs.\n- Phase 3 (interaction check): 3×3 cross-product of the top-3 neighbourhood values from Phases 1–2 (≈9 runs, plus 1 duplicate = 10) to test whether ΔPPL is additive and whether the joint optimum sits off the axes.\n- Phase 4 (optional, only if bit-width confirmed): 5-point coarse α grid at the second bit-width setting to test whether \"the optimal fixed ratio\" is bit-width dependent.\n- Total default budget ≈ 29 quantized evals + controls, parallelizable across 224 CPUs as independent processes.\n- Refinement rule: if the argmin is interior and neighbours differ by < 1 bootstrap SE, add a ±0.01 half-step refinement grid (3 extra points) rather than a full re-sweep.\n\n**4. Bit-width (explicit uncertainty — not silently chosen).** Primary proposal: **W8A8-style 8-bit symmetric input/activation quantization, 4-bit asymmetric KV** (with **2-bit asymmetric KV** as the second, more decision-relevant point, since clipping-ratio effects are largest and most cited at ≤4-bit KV). I will not treat either as settled: the answer to \"the optimal fixed ratio\" is bit-width dependent, so I need your `setup_*` topics to confirm KV bits {2,4,8} and input bits {4,8}, plus group size, K/V granularity, and sink-token policy. Until confirmed, the deliverable states the optimum as a function α*(bits, granularity) rather than a single number.\n\n**5. Eval protocol.** Primary: WikiText-2 test split, **non-overlapping 2048-token** sequences (all ~2,200 sequences → ~1.09M tokens), seq-level PPL = exp(Σ NLL / Σ tokens) with the first token of each block masked and no sliding-window overlap (a sliding-2048 variant reported as a sensitivity check because context truncation can mask KV-quantization damage). Secondary: C4 validation, 256 fixed blocks of 2048 (same block IDs across all runs). Deterministic single pass, fp16 compute, batch size 1, fixed seed; weights loaded once per run.\n\n**6. Controls / anchors (5).**\n- (C1) FP16 baseline, no quantization → reference PPL and noise floor.\n- (C2) α = 1.00 both factors (pure \"no clipping\", plain min/max–absmax) → the \"hard-clip only at true extremes\" extreme.\n- (C3) hard-clip extremes α = 0.70 and α = 0.60 (deliberate over-clipping) → confirm the degradation branch and monotonic behaviour.\n- (C4) MSE-optimal *static* per-group clip (Lloyd-Max/MSE-solver offline) → oracle upper reference and a runtime-vs-static gap statement.\n- (C5) Sanity harness controls: identity fake-quant (α=1, b=16 bits) must reproduce C1 within ~1e-3 PPL; weight-only quantization off; per-sequence fp16 NLL cached once to enable paired tests.\n\n**7. Analysis plan.**\n- Primary plot: ΔPPL (and PPL) vs α, per factor, with bootstrap 95% CIs over sequences (10k resamples), one curve per bit-width.\n- Attribution: additive model ΔPPL(α_in, α_KV) ≈ f_in(α_in) + f_KV(α_KV); report R² of additivity and the residual interaction matrix from Phase 3 → sensitivity attribution (which factor's clipping dominates, per bit-width), plus per-segment diagnostics: clipped-mass, normalized quantization MSE (NMSE), and outlier-clip event counts captured in a lightweight instrumentation pass.\n- Optimum definition + tie-breaking (pre-registered): α* = argmin of CI-bounded PPL; if multiple α are statistically indistinguishable (paired per-sequence NLL difference, bootstrap CI includes 0 / p > 0.05 after Holm correction across the grid), pick the **largest** α inside the indistinguishable set (least quantization distortion, largest headroom for lower bits, more robust across the two datasets); require α* to be interior (not a grid endpoint) and consistent between WikiText-2 and C4, else report a Pareto range rather than a single number. Report the flat-region width (how far α can move for <1% PPL change) as the practically useful \"balance\" answer.\n\n## C. Feasibility in this no-GPU / CPU-only sandbox\n\n- Nothing can be run *today*: torch/transformers/datasets/tokenizers/numpy are absent and there is no network, so the 7B weights and WikiText-2/C4 cannot be fetched; the Llama-2 licence gate is an additional real-world blocker (fallback: `NousResearch/Llama-2-7b-hf` mirror, flagged as a weights-provenance caveat).\n- CPU-only execution of a fake-quant 7B fp16 forward over ~1.1M tokens is the dominant cost; on 224 cores with 4 threads per run and ~50 concurrent runs, one 2048-block WikiText-2 pass is realistically tens of minutes to a few hours, so ~30 runs fit in hours-to-a-day *if* dependencies and weights exist. Peak memory is fine (2 TB), tmpfs is fine for activations if we store only scalars/NMSE, not full tensors.\n- Mitigations to keep the design executable elsewhere or at reduced scale: (i) pilot on a Llama-architecture toy model (2–4 layers, random weights) purely to validate hooks and the PPL harness; (ii) reduced eval (256 WikiText-2 blocks) for grid coarsening, full eval only for final α*; (iii) layer-subset quantization for the instrumentation pass.\n- If execution stays impossible, the deliverable is an analysis-ready design plus the harness code skeleton and pre-registered analysis spec — I will not claim any measured PPL.\n\n## D. Explicit assumptions vs. open uncertainties\n\nAssumptions (my defaults, stated so they can be overridden): α multiplies the runtime absmax (symmetric) / half-range (asymmetric) statistic per quantization group; fake-quant in fp16 (numerical effect only, no INT kernel); weights left in fp16 for the primary runs (weight quantization is a confounder); PPL computed on next-token prediction with first-token-per-block masked; single deterministic run (quantization is deterministic, so no seeds needed); 2048 context; WikiText-2 primary, C4 secondary; RES=128 sink tokens unquantized.\n\nOpen uncertainties needing your input before I write the full design:\n1. **Bit-width** — KV bits {2,4,8} and input/activation bits {4,8}; which combination is \"the\" primary result? (Biggest conclusion-changing unknown.)\n2. Group size / granularity (per-token, per-channel for K, per-token for V, group_size 32/64/128) and whether weights are quantized at all.\n3. Whether \"input quantization\" means the model's embedding input, all activation inputs to linear layers, or per-layer input activations.\n4. Sequence length and PPL convention (non-overlapping 2048 vs sliding window) — this materially affects KV-quantization sensitivity.\n5. Whether \"optimal fixed clipping ratio\" should be reported as a single scalar (forcing one bit-width/granularity choice) or as α*(bits, granularity) with a flat-region range — I propose the latter plus a headline scalar at the confirmed primary config.\n6. Whether the `neurips2024/94328/2:setup_{1,10,17}` topics prescribe specific values I should adopt verbatim (I cannot read them in-sandbox).\n7. Execution expectation: should the design be written for a CPU-only fallback (reduced eval) or assume GPU availability elsewhere?\n\nRequesting your approval of this approach (and answers on items 1–4, 6) before I produce the full stage=`result` design.", "evidence": "Repository/environment inspection (all commands run in-sandbox, successful but exploratory only — no experiment performed):\n1. `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` → only `/workspace/task.json`; `ls -la /workspace` → single 574-byte file. No quantization/clipping code, no Llama hooks, no datasets, no eval scripts, no git metadata.\n2. `cat /workspace/task.json` → question text identical to the public task; `\"documents\": {\"track.md\": \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"}`; datasets list empty; gpu_enabled false.\n3. `python3 -c \"import torch, transformers\"` → ModuleNotFoundError; `python3 -c \"import numpy\"` → ModuleNotFoundError; `pip list | grep -iE \"torch|numpy|transformers|datasets|sentencepiece|accelerate|scikit\"` → empty. Python 3.14.4.\n4. No network: `urllib.request.urlopen('https://pypi.org/simple/')` → URLError \"Temporary failure in name resolution\"; `pip download numpy` → fails. Therefore no dependency or model/dataset download path exists in-sandbox.\n5. `nproc` → 224; `free -g` → ~2011 GB RAM, 0 swap; `df -h /tmp` → 1006G tmpfs, 0 used; `gcc --version` → 15.2.0. No GPU mentioned by the task environment.\n6. `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname \"*neurips*\" -o -iname \"*94328*\"` → no matches; `/opt`, `/srv`, `/data`, `/mnt` do not exist → the neurips2024 setup topic docs are not available locally, so source-specific setup details must be supplied by the researcher.", "assumptions": ["The clipping ratio alpha is applied multiplicatively to the runtime statistic (absmax for symmetric inputs, half-range around center for asymmetric KV) per quantization group; alternative MAD/percentile parameterizations are robustness variants mapped onto the same alpha axis.", "Weights stay in fp16 in the primary configuration so that clipping-ratio effects on activations and KV are not confounded by weight quantization error.", "Fake-quant (quantize then dequantize, fp16 arithmetic) represents the quantization error; no custom INT kernel is needed to measure PPL.", "Primary bit-width defaults (8-bit symmetric inputs, 4-bit asymmetric KV, with 2-bit KV as second point) are my proposal, NOT task-fixed; they are flagged as the main unresolved choice.", "Eval defaults: WikiText-2 test with non-overlapping 2048-token blocks, C4 validation 256 blocks as secondary; PPL = exp(sum NLL / sum predicted tokens) with first token of each block masked.", "First 128 tokens/positions (attention sinks) are kept unquantized in the KV cache, following common KV-quantization practice.", "Single deterministic run per configuration is sufficient because fake-quant is deterministic; statistical uncertainty is quantified by bootstrap over eval sequences, not seeds.", "LLAMA2-7B refers to Llama-2-7B in HuggingFace format (licence-gated; a community mirror may be required).", "No experiment will be executed in this sandbox; deliverable is a design only."], "uncertainties": ["Bit-width is not pinned by the task: KV cache bits (2 / 4 / 8) and input-activation bits (4 / 8) materially change the optimal clipping ratio; a single 'optimal fixed ratio' is only meaningful once fixed.", "Quantization granularity and group size (per-token vs per-group for inputs; per-channel K, per-token V; group_size 32/64/128) are unspecified and affect both PPL and the optimum.", "Meaning of 'input quantization' (model embedding input vs all activation inputs to linear layers) is ambiguous.", "Sequence length and PPL convention (non-overlapping 2048 vs sliding-window) are unspecified; context truncation can hide KV-quantization damage.", "Whether the neurips2024/94328/2:setup_1/10/17 topics prescribe specific values (bit-widths, datasets, sequence length, sink-token handling) that must be adopted verbatim - these documents are not readable in the sandbox.", "Whether the 'optimal' answer should be a single scalar or a bit-width/granularity-dependent curve with a flat-region range; I propose reporting both.", "Execution feasibility: with no numpy/torch/transformers and no network, the experiment cannot run here at all; it needs a GPU or CPU host with dependencies and Llama-2 weights plus WikiText-2/C4 available offline.", "Whether weight quantization is intended to coexist with activation/KV quantization in the target deployment setting.", "Expected runtime of ~30 fp16 7B evaluation passes on CPU-only hardware is estimated, not measured."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9f1f5808a0136cf4", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-9f1f5808a0136cf4", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"stage\": \"plan\", \"answer\": \"## A. What is actually in /workspace (verified)\\n\\n- `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` \\u2192 only `/workspace/task.json`. **No quantization implementation, no clipping/ratio code, no Llama hooks/monkey-patches, no datasets, no perplexity eval scripts, no configs, no git history.** The repo is empty apart from the task statement.\\n- `task.json` mirrors the public question verbatim and adds `\\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"`\\n- Toolchain: `python3` = 3.14.4; `import torch` and `import numpy` both `ModuleNotFoundError`; `pip list` shows only OS/Ansible/apt-era packages (no numpy, scipy, torch, transformers, datasets, tokenizers, sentencepiece, accelerate).\\n- No network: DNS resolution fails (`Temporary failure in name resolution`), `pip download numpy` fails. So neither dependencies nor `meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf` weights nor WikiText-2/C4 can be obtained in-sandbox.\\n- Compute: `nproc` = 224, RAM \\u2248 2011 GB, swap 0, `/tmp` tmpfs \\u2248 1 TB, gcc 15.2 present. No GPU.\\n- No local copy of the `neurips2024/94328/2:setup_{1,10,17}` topic docs was found (searched `/` for `*neurips*`, `*94328*`), so any source-specific setup details must come from you.\\n\\nConclusion: this is a from-scratch design; nothing can be executed here, consistent with the design-only framing.\\n\\n## B. Proposed experiment design (plan, not yet full design doc)\\n\\n**Fixed by the task (non-negotiable):** model = LLAMA2-7B; KV-cache quantization is **asymmetric** (zero-point/min-max form); input quantization is **symmetric** (absmax, no zero point); metric = perplexity; the manipulated variable is the **runtime clipping ratio**.\\n\\n**1. Rationale / hypothesis.** Runtime (dynamic, per-tensor-group) clipping rescales the quantization grid below the observed extremum: too large (\\u03b1\\u21921) wastes code on outliers \\u2192 large uniform-step error; too small (\\u03b1\\u226a1) truncates outliers \\u2192 bias error. PPL should therefore be U-shaped (usually right-skewed, near-flat then sharp degradation) in \\u03b1, with the optimum shifted *lower* as bit-width decreases and *more* pronounced for asymmetric KV quantization, because asymmetric min/max is more outlier-sensitive than symmetric absmax. Activation outliers (massive-activation layers) and attention-sink tokens in K are expected to dominate sensitivity, which the design isolates via decoupled sweeps.\\n\\n**2. Quantizer definitions (one code path, fake-quant/dequantize in fp so PPL is the only moving part).**\\n- Inputs (embedding/activation inputs to quantized ops): symmetric uniform, scale s = \\u03b1 \\u00b7 max|x| / (2^(b\\u22121) \\u2212 1), round-to-nearest, group granularity per-token \\u00d7 group_size g (default g = group-along-channels; g flagged as uncertainty).\\n- KV cache: asymmetric uniform with clipped bounds [\\u2113, u] shrunk by \\u03b1 toward the group center: \\u2113\\u2032 = c \\u2212 \\u03b1(c \\u2212 \\u2113), u\\u2032 = c + \\u03b1(u \\u2212 c), c = (\\u2113+u)/2; K quantized per-channel, V per-token (both flagged; keep first `RES=128` attention-sink tokens/positions in fp16 \\u2014 flagged).\\n- \\u03b1 applied as a **multiplicative clip ratio on the runtime statistic** (primary variant, \\\"scaled-max/scaled-range\\\"). Two alternative ratio families to test as robustness: **MAD-based** (\\u03b1\\u2194\\u03c3\\u00b7k, k\\u2208{3,3.5,4,4.5,5,\\u221e}) and **percentile-based** (q\\u2208{99.99, 99.9, 99.5, 99.0, 95.0}) \\u2014 mapped onto the \\u03b1 axis by the equivalent clipping mass so curves are comparable.\\n\\n**3. Sweep structure \\u2014 decoupled first (as you requested), then a small joint block.**\\n- Phase 1 (KV frozen at \\u03b1_KV = 1.0): \\u03b1_in \\u2208 {1.00, 0.99, 0.98, 0.96, 0.94, 0.92, 0.90, 0.85, 0.80} \\u2192 9 runs.\\n- Phase 2 (input frozen at \\u03b1_in = 1.0): \\u03b1_KV \\u2208 same 9-point grid \\u2192 9 runs.\\n- Phase 3 (interaction check): 3\\u00d73 cross-product of the top-3 neighbourhood values from Phases 1\\u20132 (\\u22489 runs, plus 1 duplicate = 10) to test whether \\u0394PPL is additive and whether the joint optimum sits off the axes.\\n- Phase 4 (optional, only if bit-width confirmed): 5-point coarse \\u03b1 grid at the second bit-width setting to test whether \\\"the optimal fixed ratio\\\" is bit-width dependent.\\n- Total default budget \\u2248 29 quantized evals + controls, parallelizable across 224 CPUs as independent processes.\\n- Refinement rule: if the argmin is interior and neighbours differ by < 1 bootstrap SE, add a \\u00b10.01 half-step refinement grid (3 extra points) rather than a full re-sweep.\\n\\n**4. Bit-width (explicit uncertainty \\u2014 not silently chosen).** Primary proposal: **W8A8-style 8-bit symmetric input/activation quantization, 4-bit asymmetric KV** (with **2-bit asymmetric KV** as the second, more decision-relevant point, since clipping-ratio effects are largest and most cited at \\u22644-bit KV). I will not treat either as settled: the answer to \\\"the optimal fixed ratio\\\" is bit-width dependent, so I need your `setup_*` topics to confirm KV bits {2,4,8} and input bits {4,8}, plus group size, K/V granularity, and sink-token policy. Until confirmed, the deliverable states the optimum as a function \\u03b1*(bits, granularity) rather than a single number.\\n\\n**5. Eval protocol.** Primary: WikiText-2 test split, **non-overlapping 2048-token** sequences (all ~2,200 sequences \\u2192 ~1.09M tokens), seq-level PPL = exp(\\u03a3 NLL / \\u03a3 tokens) with the first token of each block masked and no sliding-window overlap (a sliding-2048 variant reported as a sensitivity check because context truncation can mask KV-quantization damage). Secondary: C4 validation, 256 fixed blocks of 2048 (same block IDs across all runs). Deterministic single pass, fp16 compute, batch size 1, fixed seed; weights loaded once per run.\\n\\n**6. Controls / anchors (5).**\\n- (C1) FP16 baseline, no quantization \\u2192 reference PPL and noise floor.\\n- (C2) \\u03b1 = 1.00 both factors (pure \\\"no clipping\\\", plain min/max\\u2013absmax) \\u2192 the \\\"hard-clip only at true extremes\\\" extreme.\\n- (C3) hard-clip extremes \\u03b1 = 0.70 and \\u03b1 = 0.60 (deliberate over-clipping) \\u2192 confirm the degradation branch and monotonic behaviour.\\n- (C4) MSE-optimal *static* per-group clip (Lloyd-Max/MSE-solver offline) \\u2192 oracle upper reference and a runtime-vs-static gap statement.\\n- (C5) Sanity harness controls: identity fake-quant (\\u03b1=1, b=16 bits) must reproduce C1 within ~1e-3 PPL; weight-only quantization off; per-sequence fp16 NLL cached once to enable paired tests.\\n\\n**7. Analysis plan.**\\n- Primary plot: \\u0394PPL (and PPL) vs \\u03b1, per factor, with bootstrap 95% CIs over sequences (10k resamples), one curve per bit-width.\\n- Attribution: additive model \\u0394PPL(\\u03b1_in, \\u03b1_KV) \\u2248 f_in(\\u03b1_in) + f_KV(\\u03b1_KV); report R\\u00b2 of additivity and the residual interaction matrix from Phase 3 \\u2192 sensitivity attribution (which factor's clipping dominates, per bit-width), plus per-segment diagnostics: clipped-mass, normalized quantization MSE (NMSE), and outlier-clip event counts captured in a lightweight instrumentation pass.\\n- Optimum definition + tie-breaking (pre-registered): \\u03b1* = argmin of CI-bounded PPL; if multiple \\u03b1 are statistically indistinguishable (paired per-sequence NLL difference, bootstrap CI includes 0 / p > 0.05 after Holm correction across the grid), pick the **largest** \\u03b1 inside the indistinguishable set (least quantization distortion, largest headroom for lower bits, more robust across the two datasets); require \\u03b1* to be interior (not a grid endpoint) and consistent between WikiText-2 and C4, else report a Pareto range rather than a single number. Report the flat-region width (how far \\u03b1 can move for <1% PPL change) as the practically useful \\\"balance\\\" answer.\\n\\n## C. Feasibility in this no-GPU / CPU-only sandbox\\n\\n- Nothing can be run *today*: torch/transformers/datasets/tokenizers/numpy are absent and there is no network, so the 7B weights and WikiText-2/C4 cannot be fetched; the Llama-2 licence gate is an additional real-world blocker (fallback: `NousResearch/Llama-2-7b-hf` mirror, flagged as a weights-provenance caveat).\\n- CPU-only execution of a fake-quant 7B fp16 forward over ~1.1M tokens is the dominant cost; on 224 cores with 4 threads per run and ~50 concurrent runs, one 2048-block WikiText-2 pass is realistically tens of minutes to a few hours, so ~30 runs fit in hours-to-a-day *if* dependencies and weights exist. Peak memory is fine (2 TB), tmpfs is fine for activations if we store only scalars/NMSE, not full tensors.\\n- Mitigations to keep the design executable elsewhere or at reduced scale: (i) pilot on a Llama-architecture toy model (2\\u20134 layers, random weights) purely to validate hooks and the PPL harness; (ii) reduced eval (256 WikiText-2 blocks) for grid coarsening, full eval only for final \\u03b1*; (iii) layer-subset quantization for the instrumentation pass.\\n- If execution stays impossible, the deliverable is an analysis-ready design plus the harness code skeleton and pre-registered analysis spec \\u2014 I will not claim any measured PPL.\\n\\n## D. Explicit assumptions vs. open uncertainties\\n\\nAssumptions (my defaults, stated so they can be overridden): \\u03b1 multiplies the runtime absmax (symmetric) / half-range (asymmetric) statistic per quantization group; fake-quant in fp16 (numerical effect only, no INT kernel); weights left in fp16 for the primary runs (weight quantization is a confounder); PPL computed on next-token prediction with first-token-per-block masked; single deterministic run (quantization is deterministic, so no seeds needed); 2048 context; WikiText-2 primary, C4 secondary; RES=128 sink tokens unquantized.\\n\\nOpen uncertainties needing your input before I write the full design:\\n1. **Bit-width** \\u2014 KV bits {2,4,8} and input/activation bits {4,8}; which combination is \\\"the\\\" primary result? (Biggest conclusion-changing unknown.)\\n2. Group size / granularity (per-token, per-channel for K, per-token for V, group_size 32/64/128) and whether weights are quantized at all.\\n3. Whether \\\"input quantization\\\" means the model's embedding input, all activation inputs to linear layers, or per-layer input activations.\\n4. Sequence length and PPL convention (non-overlapping 2048 vs sliding window) \\u2014 this materially affects KV-quantization sensitivity.\\n5. Whether \\\"optimal fixed clipping ratio\\\" should be reported as a single scalar (forcing one bit-width/granularity choice) or as \\u03b1*(bits, granularity) with a flat-region range \\u2014 I propose the latter plus a headline scalar at the confirmed primary config.\\n6. Whether the `neurips2024/94328/2:setup_{1,10,17}` topics prescribe specific values I should adopt verbatim (I cannot read them in-sandbox).\\n7. Execution expectation: should the design be written for a CPU-only fallback (reduced eval) or assume GPU availability elsewhere?\\n\\nRequesting your approval of this approach (and answers on items 1\\u20134, 6) before I produce the full stage=`result` design.\", \"evidence\": \"Repository/environment inspection (all commands run in-sandbox, successful but exploratory only \\u2014 no experiment performed):\\n1. `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` \\u2192 only `/workspace/task.json`; `ls -la /workspace` \\u2192 single 574-byte file. No quantization/clipping code, no Llama hooks, no datasets, no eval scripts, no git metadata.\\n2. `cat /workspace/task.json` \\u2192 question text identical to the public task; `\\\"documents\\\": {\\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"}`; datasets list empty; gpu_enabled false.\\n3. `python3 -c \\\"import torch, transformers\\\"` \\u2192 ModuleNotFoundError; `python3 -c \\\"import numpy\\\"` \\u2192 ModuleNotFoundError; `pip list | grep -iE \\\"torch|numpy|transformers|datasets|sentencepiece|accelerate|scikit\\\"` \\u2192 empty. Python 3.14.4.\\n4. No network: `urllib.request.urlopen('https://pypi.org/simple/')` \\u2192 URLError \\\"Temporary failure in name resolution\\\"; `pip download numpy` \\u2192 fails. Therefore no dependency or model/dataset download path exists in-sandbox.\\n5. `nproc` \\u2192 224; `free -g` \\u2192 ~2011 GB RAM, 0 swap; `df -h /tmp` \\u2192 1006G tmpfs, 0 used; `gcc --version` \\u2192 15.2.0. No GPU mentioned by the task environment.\\n6. `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname \\\"*neurips*\\\" -o -iname \\\"*94328*\\\"` \\u2192 no matches; `/opt`, `/srv`, `/data`, `/mnt` do not exist \\u2192 the neurips2024 setup topic docs are not available locally, so source-specific setup details must be supplied by the researcher.\", \"assumptions\": [\"The clipping ratio alpha is applied multiplicatively to the runtime statistic (absmax for symmetric inputs, half-range around center for asymmetric KV) per quantization group; alternative MAD/percentile parameterizations are robustness variants mapped onto the same alpha axis.\", \"Weights stay in fp16 in the primary configuration so that clipping-ratio effects on activations and KV are not confounded by weight quantization error.\", \"Fake-quant (quantize then dequantize, fp16 arithmetic) represents the quantization error; no custom INT kernel is needed to measure PPL.\", \"Primary bit-width defaults (8-bit symmetric inputs, 4-bit asymmetric KV, with 2-bit KV as second point) are my proposal, NOT task-fixed; they are flagged as the main unresolved choice.\", \"Eval defaults: WikiText-2 test with non-overlapping 2048-token blocks, C4 validation 256 blocks as secondary; PPL = exp(sum NLL / sum predicted tokens) with first token of each block masked.\", \"First 128 tokens/positions (attention sinks) are kept unquantized in the KV cache, following common KV-quantization practice.\", \"Single deterministic run per configuration is sufficient because fake-quant is deterministic; statistical uncertainty is quantified by bootstrap over eval sequences, not seeds.\", \"LLAMA2-7B refers to Llama-2-7B in HuggingFace format (licence-gated; a community mirror may be required).\", \"No experiment will be executed in this sandbox; deliverable is a design only.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Bit-width is not pinned by the task: KV cache bits (2 / 4 / 8) and input-activation bits (4 / 8) materially change the optimal clipping ratio; a single 'optimal fixed ratio' is only meaningful once fixed.\", \"Quantization granularity and group size (per-token vs per-group for inputs; per-channel K, per-token V; group_size 32/64/128) are unspecified and affect both PPL and the optimum.\", \"Meaning of 'input quantization' (model embedding input vs all activation inputs to linear layers) is ambiguous.\", \"Sequence length and PPL convention (non-overlapping 2048 vs sliding-window) are unspecified; context truncation can hide KV-quantization damage.\", \"Whether the neurips2024/94328/2:setup_1/10/17 topics prescribe specific values (bit-widths, datasets, sequence length, sink-token handling) that must be adopted verbatim - these documents are not readable in the sandbox.\", \"Whether the 'optimal' answer should be a single scalar or a bit-width/granularity-dependent curve with a flat-region range; I propose reporting both.\", \"Execution feasibility: with no numpy/torch/transformers and no network, the experiment cannot run here at all; it needs a GPU or CPU host with dependencies and Llama-2 weights plus WikiText-2/C4 available offline.\", \"Whether weight quantization is intended to coexist with activation/KV quantization in the target deployment setting.\", \"Expected runtime of ~30 fp16 7B evaluation passes on CPU-only hardware is estimated, not measured.\"], \"summary\": \"Inspect empty repo and propose decoupled clipping-ratio PPL sweep design\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-c36e8a51-633a-4653-a717-fee41b1585d7", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Inspect empty repo and propose decoupled clipping-ratio PPL sweep design", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
