tion of Phase 2 at α ∈ {0.95,0.90,0.85}; sliding-window at α*; C4).\n5. Eval protocol: WikiText-2 test, non-overlapping 2048, PPL definition formula, ~120 blocks (verify at prep), C4 256 fixed block IDs, deterministic.\n6. Controls C1–C5 + C6 sink control (RES=0), C7 granularity ablation.\n7. Instrumentation: NMSE, clipped mass, effective bits, sink-clip rate; scalar-only storage.\n8. Analysis spec: ΔPPL curves, paired per-sequence bootstrap CI (10k resamples), additive model + interaction residual matrix, Holm correction, pre-registered α* rule verbatim, plateau width (<1% ΔPPL), Pareto fallback, attribution split.\n9. Deliverables & run manifest (table of ~56 runs).\n10. Feasibility: sandbox = zero executability (no numpy/torch/network/weights/datasets); CPU-hour and GPU-hour estimates with derivation; fallbacks (toy Llama harness debug, 64-block coarse grid); explicit statement that these are analytic estimates, not measurements.\n11. Assumptions vs uncertainties lists.\n12. Limitations: group_size 32/64 untested variability, weight quantization out of scope, single model size, PPL-only, fake-quant vs true kernel, licence/weights provenance.\n\nAlso gate conditions: C5 identity fake-quant as harness validity gate (must be met before any grid run), plus pre-registration note.\n\nLet me count runs precisely and build the manifest table:\n\nPrimary config (IN=8b sym, KV=4b asym), WT2-2048:\n- C1 FP16 baseline: 1\n- C2 α=1.0/1.0: 1\n- C5 identity fake-quant gate: 1\n- Phase 1: α_in ∈ {0.99,0.98,0.96,0.94,0.92,0.90,0.85,0.80} with α_KV=1.0 → 8 (α_in=1.0 = C2)\n- Phase 2: α_KV ∈ {0.99,0.98,0.96,0.94,0.92,0.90,0.85,0.80} with α_in=1.0 → 8\n- Phase 3: 3×3 cross around best; exclude already-run α=1.0 rows? Keep 9 (dedupe → up to 9)\n- C3 extremes α_in=α_KV=0.70, 0.60 (both factors clipped): 2\n- C4 MSE-optimal static clip: 2 (one with α-style applied to inputs, one to KV) — simpler: 1 run with MSE-optimal static clip on both, plus 1 with inputs only? Keep 2.\n- C7 granularity ablation K per-token at α_KV=0.95: 1\n- C6 sink control RES=0 at α_KV ∈ {0.95,0.90,0.85}: 3\n- MAD/percentile parameterization points: 4 (2 MAD equivalents + 2 percentile equivalents at mid-range α_equiv, one per factor)\n- refinement (conditional): 3\nPrimary subtotal ≈ 34 (+3 conditional)\n\n2-bit KV config:\n- C2' α=1.0/1.0 at KV2b: 1\n- Phase1' α_in grid 8, Phase2' α_KV grid 8, interaction 3×3 → 9 (or 2×2=4 to save); RES=0 check 3; = 21-25. Let's specify 9-point α_in grid? To control budget: 7-point α_in (subset), 7-point α_KV, 2×2 interaction (4), 3 RES=0 checks → 1+6+6+4+3 = 20.\n\nCross-cutting:\n- C4 dataset (WikiText-2 vs C4): C1 FP16 on C4 (1) + α* configs on C4 (2: primary and 2-bit) + no-clip α=1.0 on C4 (2) → 5\n- sliding-window sensitivity at α* (primary cfg): 1\n- toy-Llama harness debug runs: 3 (free, tiny)\n\nTotal ≈ 34 + 20 + 5 + 1 ≈ 60 quantized 7B runs (+3 conditional refinement, +3 toy). Say ~56–63. Cost earlier: use 56-63 runs.\n\nI'll present the manifest with counts and totals ~63 runs (≈60 primary + conditional).\n\nCost: WT2 run ~246k tokens ≈ 3.4 PFLOP forward. CPU realistic: 7B forward-only bf16/fp16 on 224-core EPYC with MKL/OpenBLAS — achievable effective TFLOPs maybe 5–20 TFLOPS with AMX bf16 on Sapphire Rapids; on fp16 without AMX, much lower (0.5–2). I'll present range 1–10 TFLOPS effective → 0.1–1 h/run. Plus fake-quant overhead 1.5–3× (memory-bound group reductions; V has 256M groups per eval → significant). Present a range: optimistic 0.15 h/run, expected 0.5 h/run, pessimistic 3 h/run. Total ~60 runs → 9–30 expected run-hours serial with parallelism; worst case 180 run-hours → ~20 h wall at 8-way concurrency. Note memory-bandwidth and thread-scaling inefficiency: fake-quant on CPU is memory bound; recommend 16-32 threads per run, 4-8 concurrent runs.\n\nGPU: 3.4 PFLOP/run at 60 TFLOP/s effective = 3.4 min analytic; with fake-quant overhead ×3 → ~10 min/run → 60 runs ≈ 10 GPU-h; plus weights load overhead. Say 5–15 GPU-hours single A100-80GB (fp16 13.5 GB + KV 1.1 GB/block-context).\n\nAlso note: no INT kernel needed; fake-quant makes cost independent of bit-width, so 2-bit runs cost same as 8-bit.\n\nAlso, a real speedup trick: cache FP16 per-sequence NLL once (C1) for paired tests — no re-run needed.\n\nAlso recommend \"quantization-only instrumentation pass\" to capture group statistics for NMSE/clipped-mass: can be done in the same run, scalar-only.\n\nAlso: because fake-quant cost is α-independent, the whole grid costs the same as any other grid — hence \"cheap sweep\" argument.\n\nImportant methodological note: for KV clipping, α also affects only the stored cache; but attention sink tokens in fp16 (RES window) are never clipped → the RES=0 control isolates that.\n\nAlso pre-registration: no peeking/adaptive grid changes except the pre-specified refinement rule.\n\nStatistical power note: only ~120 blocks; bootstrap over blocks; use paired differences to reduce variance (same blocks). Expected ΔPPL at 8-bit inputs ~0.01–0.1; with 120 blocks paired SE maybe ~0.02–0.05 → flag that plateau criterion is robust to this, and if the whole grid is statistically indistinguishable from FP16, the honest answer is \"PPL is insensitive to α over [α_min, 1.0]; report α_min where degradation exceeds threshold\". That's a great pre-registered outcome branch: define α_min = smallest α with ΔPPL below threshold; then α* = largest α in the indistinguishable set → equals 1.0 unless clipping helps. Interesting: often α<1 helps only at low bits. Add pre-registered outcome branches:\n (a) monotone degradation (α=1 best) → answer: no clipping is optimal; report α where degradation crosses 1%.\n (b) interior optimum (U-shaped) → report α*.\n (c) flat within noise → report plateau + α_min.\n\nInclude tie-breaking verbatim as approved: \"largest α inside the statistically indistinguishable set, interior requirement, WikiText-2/C4 agreement, else Pareto range\".\n\nNow write the final answer. It should be thorough but organized. I'll keep formulas in plain text.\n\nI also want to note the correction of my earlier token-count error explicitly (transparency).\n\nLength: substantial. Let's write.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# EXPERIMENT DESIGN — Effect of runtime clipping ratio on LLAMA2-7B perplexity (symmetric input quantization + asymmetric KV-cache quantization)\n\nDesign-only artifact. **No experiment was run; no perplexity values in this document are measured.** All PPL quantities are unknowns to be filled by execution.\n\n## 0. Provenance of every setup choice (three tiers — do not conflate)\n\n- **T0 · Task-fixed (requirements):** model = LLAMA2-7B; KV-cache quantization is **asymmetric**; metric = **perplexity**; manipulated variable = runtime clipping ratio.\n- **T1 · Researcher-set decisions (approved 2026-10-22; these are *assumptions*, not source facts):** 8-bit symmetric input/activation quantization (primary) + 4-bit asymmetric KV; second decisive config 2-bit asymmetric KV; no 4-bit-input round; inputs = activation inputs to quantized linear layers (not embedding-only); per-token × group128 inputs, K per-channel / V per-token, group 128; weights fp16; RES=128 sink tokens kept fp16; non-overlapping 2048 primary with sliding-window as a 1-run sensitivity check at α*; WikiText-2 primary + C4 256 fixed blocks; decoupled sweeps then 3×3 interaction; MSE-optimal static oracle and identity fake-quant mandatory (the latter as a **hard validity gate**); pre-registered tie-breaking approved verbatim; \"optimal\" delivered as α\\*(bits, granularity) + one headline scalar for the primary config + plateau width (<1 % ΔPPL).\n- **T2 · My defaults (overridable):** α parameterization details, grid values, thresholds (1 % ΔPPL), bootstrap settings, diagnostic definitions, run manifest, cost model.\n- **Source status:** the only source-supported setup content available is the T0 triple. Per your confirmation, `neurips2024/94328/2:setup_{1,10,17}` adds nothing about bits, dataset or sink policy, and nothing relevant was found locally (`find / -maxdepth 4 -iname '*neurips*' -o -iname '*94328*'` → no matches). Everything outside T0 is therefore marked as assumption throughout.\n\n## 1. Rationale\n\nRuntime clipping trades two opposing errors within one scalar knob. Shrinking the quantization interval below the observed extremum reduces the uniform step Δ (variance/rounding error ∝ Δ²) but introduces truncation bias on every element outside the clipped bound. Because LLM activation and K-cache distributions are heavy-tailed (a few outlier channels dominate absmax), the *optimal* bound sits below the true extremum, and how far below depends on (i) bit-width, (b) the granularity over which the statistic is computed, and (c) whether the quantizer is symmetric (absmax, no zero point) or asymmetric (min/max zero-point, more outlier-sensitive because *both* bounds are estimated from data). Asymmetric KV quantization is expected to be the more α-sensitive factor at ≤4 bits, since it uses a single min/max pair per group for the whole group and K has known massive channels plus attention-sink structure. The experiment is designed to (1) resolve the shape of PPL(α) for each factor separately, (2) attribute sensitivity to the correct factor, (3) separate \"clipping cuts outliers\" from \"clipping cuts attention sinks\", and (4) deliver the practical answer as a **plateau** rather than a point.\n\nExpected outcome shapes, pre-declared (see §7.5 for how each is reported): (a) monotone (α=1 best → no clipping is optimal, report the α where degradation crosses 1 %); (b) interior U-shaped optimum (report α*); (c) flat within noise (report plateau + smallest α meeting the threshold).\n\n## 2. Quantizer formalization and the α axis\n\nAll quantization is **fake-quant** (quantize→dequantize, fp16 arithmetic), so bit-width does not change compute cost and the only moving part is numerical error. Round-to-nearest, saturating to the code range.\n\n**2.1 Input/activation quantizer (symmetric).** For activation matrix X ∈ ℝ^{T×d}, per token t and channel-group g of size 128:\n`m_{t,g} = max_j |x_{t,g,j}|`, `b = α`, `s = b·m_{t,g} / (2^{B_in−1} − 1)`, `q = clamp(round(x/s), −(2^{B_in−1}−1), 2^{B_in−1}−1)`, `x̂ = q·s`. Zero-point implicitly 0 (symmetric). Applied to the inputs of all quantized linear layers: q/k/v/o and gate/up/down projections of every block (post-activation, dynamic, computed from the live tensor at inference time).\n\n**2.2 KV quantizer (asymmetric).** For group G (K: a channel's values over 128 consecutive tokens; V: 128 consecutive head-dim values of one token), center `c = (min+max)/2`, half-range `r = (max−min)/2`:\n`ℓ = c − b·r`, `u = c + b·r`, `s = (u−ℓ)/(2^B_KV − 1)`, `z = round(−ℓ/s)`, `q = clamp(round(x/s)+z, 0, 2^B_KV−1)`, `x̂ = (q−z)·s`. Same b for both bounds. Cache positions in the RES=128 sink window stay fp16 and are never clipped (T1).\n\n**2.3 Primary α family — scaled-max / scaled-range (S-MAX).** b = α ∈ (0,1] directly multiplying the runtime statistic. This is the primary axis; α=1 is exactly plain min/max–absmax quantization (no clipping).\n\n**2.4 Alternative families, mapped onto the same axis.**\n- **MAD/SIGMA:** bound = median + k·1.4826·MAD (symmetric: max over |·|), k ∈ {3, 3.5, 4, 4.5, 5, ∞}.\n- **PCTL:** bound = empirical q-quantile of |x| (symmetric) or of x (asymmetric), q ∈ {100, 99.99, 99.9, 99.5, 99.0, 95.0}.\n- **Mapping rule:** for each captured group, compute `α_eq = bound_S-MAX_used / absmax_or_halfrange`; the family's point is plotted at the token-count-weighted mean α_eq measured on a fixed 8-block calibration sample, with the interquartile spread of α_eq reported as error bars. This makes all three families comparable on one α axis and shows that MAD/PCTL are *group-adaptive* α's rather than different physics. Because families are group-adaptive they will not coincide exactly with any fixed α; they are reported as overlay points, never pooled into the fixed-α curve's fit.\n\n## 3. Configuration and grid\n\n**Primary config P1:** B_in = 8 (symmetric, per-token × group128), B_KV = 4 (asymmetric, K per-channel / V per-token, group128), weights fp16, RES=128.\n**Second config P2:** same but **B_KV = 2** (expected maximum clipping effect; this is the config that will decide the headline conclusion).\n\n**Sweep grid (both factors):** α ∈ {1.00, 0.99, 0.98, 0.96, 0.94, 0.92, 0.90, 0.85, 0.80} (9 points). P2 uses a 7-point subset {1.00, 0.98, 0.96, 0.94, 0.90, 0.85, 0.80}.\n\n**Phase structure (attribution tree, decoupled first):**\n- **Phase 1 — input axis:** α_in over the grid, α_KV pinned at 1.00.\n- **Phase 2 — KV axis:** α_KV over the grid, α_in pinned at 1.00.\n- **Phase 3 — interaction block:** 3×3 cross-product of the top-3 neighbourhood values from Phases 1–2 (P1 only). Tests additivity and whether the joint optimum sits off-axis.\n- **Phase 4 — P2 replication:** Phases 1–2 on the 7-point grid at B_KV=2 + a 2×2 interaction block + the RES=0 sink control.\n- **Refinement rule (pre-specified, the only adaptive step allowed):** if the grid argmin is interior and its two neighbours differ by < 1 bootstrap SE, add ±0.01 half-steps (≤3 runs). No other grid changes after any results are seen.\n\n**Run manifest (counts).** Shared: C1 FP16 baseline; C2 α=1/1 (= α_in=1.00 & α_KV=1.00 anchor); C5 identity fake-quant gate → 3. P1: Phase 1 = 8 new, Phase 2 = 8 new, Phase 3 = 9, C3 extremes (0.70, 0.60 on both factors) = 2, C4 MSE-optimal static (both-factor and input-only) = 2, C7 K per-token-vs-per-channel at α_KV=0.95 = 1, C6 RES=0 at α_KV ∈ {0.95, 0.90, 0.85} = 3, MAD/PCTL overlay points = 4 → 37. P2: anchor 1, Phase 1′ = 6, Phase 2′ = 6, 2×2 = 4, RES=0 ×3 → 17. Cross-cutting: C4-dataset (C4) runs = 5 (FP16, α=1/1, α* for P1 and P2), sliding-window 1024-stride-2048-window at α* (P1) = 1 → 6. **Total ≈ 63 runs** (+≤3 conditional refinement, +3 free toy-model debug runs).\n\n## 4. Data, PPL definition, determinism\n\n- **Primary:** WikiText-2 test split (`wikitext-2-raw-v1`), documents concatenated with BOS separators, cut into **non-overlapping 2048-token blocks**; trailing partial block dropped. Expected ≈245k tokens ≈ **120 blocks** — *this count must be verified at data-prep time* (see §9; it corrects my earlier plan, which wrongly assumed ~1.09M tokens / 2,200 sequences).\n- **Secondary:** C4 validation, first 256,000 documents streamed/tokenized → 256 blocks fixed as an explicit list of block IDs, identical in every run.\n- **PPL definition:** for block i with token sequence x₁..x_S, `NLL_i = Σ_{t=2..S} −log p(x_t | x_{<t})`, `n_i = S−1` predicted tokens (first token of each block masked; no cross-block context). `PPL = exp( (Σ_i NLL_i) / (Σ_i n_i) )` — token-weighted corpus PPL (primary). Per-block mean and geometric-mean-of-block-PPL reported as secondary summaries.\n- **Determinism:** fp16 weights, batch size 1, single forward pass per block, no sampling, fixed eval order, `torch.use_deterministic_algorithms` where applicable; quantization is deterministic → **no random seeds and no seed replicates**; all uncertainty is resampling uncertainty over blocks.\n\n## 5. Controls (full list)\n\n- **C1 · FP16 baseline** — no quantization; reference for all ΔPPL; per-block NLL vector cached **once** and reused for all paired tests (no re-runs needed).\n- **C2 · No-clip anchor α=1.00/1.00** — plain min/max & absmax; the \"no clipping\" extreme and Phase-1/2 origin.\n- **C3 · Hard-clip extremes α=0.70 and α=0.60** (both factors) — force the degradation branch, verify monotone behaviour and that the grid's low end is beyond the optimum.\n- **C4 · MSE-optimal static clip (oracle reference)** — offline per-group clip minimising quantization MSE on a calibration set, applied statically at inference; two runs (both-factor, input-only). Provides the runtime-vs-static gap and an upper reference for what any fixed dynamic α can achieve.\n- **C5 · Identity fake-quant — HARNESS VALIDITY GATE (blocking).** α=1.0 with B=16 (and the KV path likewise) must reproduce C1 within |ΔPPL| ≤ 0.005 and per-block NLL max |Δ| ≤ 1e-3. **Gate condition: if C5 fails, no grid run is executed and no result is reported** — the failure is fixed first. A second gate: a 4-bit run must degrade PPL measurably vs C1 (nonzero effect) and a no-op code path must be bit-identical to C1.\n- **C6 · Sink control (T1, new).** Phase-2 α_KV points {0.95, 0.90, 0.85} repeated with **RES=0** (sink tokens quantized like everything else). ΔPPL(RES=0) − ΔPPL(RES=128) at matched α attributes the KV-axis degradation into \"outlier clipping\" vs \"attention-sink clipping\" — without this the KV attribution at the core of the question is confounded.\n- **C7 · Granularity escape hatch (T1, one run).** K per-token instead of per-channel at α_KV=0.95, to confirm the result is not an artefact of sitting in a per-channel sensitivity pit. group_size ∈ {32, 64} are **not** run — recorded in limitations (§9).\n\n## 6. Instrumentation (cheap diagnostics, computed inside the same pass)\n\nPer quantized group record scalars only (no tensors, so storage ≈ MBs): group size, statistic used, bound, α·stat, number and fraction of clipped elements, clipped L2 mass `Σ(x−x̂)²_outlier / Σ(x−x̂)²`, quantization NMSE `Σ(x−x̂)²/Σx²`, effective step size s. Aggregate per layer × type (q/k/v/o, gate/up/down; K/V) → curves of NMSE and clipped-mass vs α, to be plotted against ΔPPL. Expected signature: NMSE has its own optimum at lower α than PPL, and K-layer NMSE dominates V — if PPL-optimal α ≠ NMSE-optimal α, that is itself a reported finding about surrogate metrics.\n\n## 7. Analysis specification\n\n**7.1 Primary plots.** ΔPPL vs α (Δ = PPL(config) − PPL(C1)) and absolute PPL vs α, one panel per factor, one panel per config (P1, P2), with 95 % bootstrap CIs; MAD/PCTL overlay points with α_eq spread bars.\n\n**7.2 Uncertainty.** Nonparametric bootstrap over blocks (10,000 resamples, seed 0) of the token-weighted PPL ratio. All config-vs-baseline comparisons are **paired over blocks** (identical block set): paired bootstrap on per-block NLL differences; two-sided p from the paired permutation/bootstrap; **Holm correction across the whole grid** (all comparisons in a family per config). Reported alongside the minimum detectable ΔPPL at 80 % power given ~120 blocks, computed before interpretation.\n\n**7.3 Additive attribution model.** Fit `ΔPPL(α_in, α_KV) ≈ f_in(α_in) + f_KV(α_KV)` (monotone-smooth interpolation of the marginal curves) on Phase-1/2 data; evaluate residuals on the Phase-3 block; report the full interaction matrix (α_in rows × α_KV columns), max |residual|, R², and an interaction F-test. Sensitivity attribution = |∂ΔPPL/∂α| at the plateau edge per factor, plus the fraction of total joint ΔPPL explained by each axis; cross-checked against per-layer NMSE (hypothesis: dominant layer group predicts the dominant axis).\n\n**7.4 Optimum + tie-breaking (approved verbatim, pre-registered).** α* = argmin of CI-bounded PPL. If several α are statistically indistinguishable (paired per-block NLL difference CI includes 0, i.e. p > 0.05 after Holm), select the **largest α in the indistinguishable set** (least quantization distortion, largest headroom, better transfer expectation). α* must be **interior** to the grid (an endpoint ⇒ extend the grid once, then re-decide) and must **agree between WikiText-2 and C4**; on disagreement, report a **Pareto range** rather than a scalar. Additionally report **plateau width**: the maximal interval of α over which ΔPPL stays within 1 % of ΔPPL(α=1.00) *and* within 1 % of ΔPPL(α*) — this interval, not the point, is the practical \"balance quantization error vs accuracy\" answer. Deliverables: α*(B_in, B_KV, granularity) table for {4-bit KV, 2-bit KV} × input axis, **one headline scalar α\\* for P1 (8-bit symmetric inputs / 4-bit asymmetric KV)** plus its plateau, and the P2 scalar with its plateau.\n\n**7.5 Outcome branches (pre-declared).** (a) monotone → \"no clipping is optimal\"; report the α where ΔPPL first exceeds 1 %. (b) interior optimum → report α* and plateau. (c) whole grid indistinguishable from C1 → report that PPL is insensitive over [α_low, 1] with the power bound from §7.2; the answer then is the smallest α with ΔPPL ≤ 1 % (max robustness headroom), explicitly framed as a non-discrimination result, not as \"clipping is free\".\n\n**7.6 Secondary checks.** Sliding-window (window 2048, stride 1024, context-preserving) at α* — if its ΔPPL differs materially from the non-overlapping estimate, the primary convention is masking KV damage and this must be stated prominently. C4 vs WikiText-2 gap → generalization of α*. RES=0 vs RES=128 split (§5 C6). Runtime-vs-static-oracle gap (§5 C4).\n\n## 8. Feasibility (analytic estimates, **not** measurements)\n\n**This sandbox: zero executability.** Verified: `/workspace` holds only `task.json` (no quantization code, no clipping logic, no Llama hooks, no datasets, no eval scripts); no `numpy`/`torch`/`transformers`/`datasets`/`tokenizers`/`sentencepiece`; DNS resolution fails and `pip download` fails → no dependency, weight (Llama-2 is licence-gated; community mirror would be a provenance caveat) or dataset acquisition. Therefore nothing in §3–§7 can run here; the design must execute on a host with those artifacts. Hardware present: 224 CPUs, ~2 TB RAM, ~1 TB tmpfs, gcc 15, **no GPU**.\n\n**Cost model (analytic, forward-only):** 2·P = 14.01 GFLOP/token plus ~4 % causal-attention term. WikiText-2 eval ≈246k tokens ≈ **3.4 PFLOP**; C4-256 ≈7.4 PFLOP; 64-block coarse subset ≈1.8 PFLOP. fp16 weights 13.5 GB; one 2048-token KV cache in fp16 ≈1.1 GB.\n- **CPU:** at 1–2 effective TFLOP/s (224-core fp16/bf16 GEMM, memory-bandwidth-limited), one full-WT2 run ≈ **0.5–1 h**, pessimistic 0.3 TF/s ≈ 3 h; ×1.5–3 fake-quant overhead (group absmax/min-max reductions; V has ~1 group per token·layer ⇒ ~2.6e8 groups per eval, so overhead is real). 63 runs ≈ **30–200 run-hours serial**; at 4–8 concurrent runs (16–32 threads each, ~55 GB RAM each — 2 TB is ample) wall clock ≈ **8–30 h**, worst case ~1–2 days. Thread-scaling is sub-linear; expect memory-bandwidth contention above ~8 concurrent runs.\n- **GPU (whichever arrives first):** 3.4 PFLOP at 60 TFLOP/s effective ≈ 3 min analytic floor, ×3 fake-quant overhead ≈ **10–20 min/run** → **~10–21 GPU-hours** for the full campaign on one 80 GB card (weights + KV fit comfortably; 2 concurrent runs feasible). GPU is ~10–30× cheaper in wall clock than the CPU fallback.\n- **Fallbacks, in order:** (1) toy-Llama harness debug (2–4 Llama-architecture layers, random init, 8 blocks) — free, validates hook wiring, α plumbing, PPL math, and satisfies gate C5 before any 7B run; (2) coarse grid on a fixed 64-block WikiText-2 subset for grid narrowing, with **full-eval confirmation required for α\\*, the plateau and every headline number** (subset results are never reported as the answer); (3) reduced C4 to 128 blocks if needed. If neither GPU nor a dep-provisioned CPU host exists, the deliverable stays a design + harness skeleton and **no PPL claim is made**.\n- These are FLOP-division estimates from an analytic FLOP count, not measurements; they will be replaced by measured timings from the toy-model and first real run before the manifest is scheduled.\n\n## 9. Limitations and untested variability\n\ngroup_size ∈ {32, 64} untested (only 128; per §5 C7 a single granularity point is tested, so granularity dependence is characterised at one point only); no 4-bit-input config, so input-bit sensitivity is a single point (8-bit) and any claim of bit-width universality for the input axis is out of reach; weights fp16 throughout — weight quantization is known to shift activation-clipping sensitivity and is out of scope; fake-quant does not exercise real INT kernels or the memory-bandwidth/throughput consequences of clipping (α has zero runtime cost effect here, so \"optimal for PPL\" ≠ \"optimal for latency/memory\"); PPL only, no zero-shot/downstream/long-generation (α tuned on PPL can be suboptimal for tasks sensitive to rare outliers); one 7B model, one seed-free deterministic protocol (no weight-sampling variance); ~120 primary blocks limits power, which is precisely why the plateau, paired tests and branch (c) are the primary reporting forms; token/block count is an expectation pending data-prep verification; α is fixed and factor-specific — per-layer or adaptive-α schedules are excluded by the question's \"fixed ratio\" framing.\n\n## 10. Assumptions vs. open uncertainties\n\n**Assumptions:** all of T0 plus everything in T1 (researcher-set) and §2/§3/§4 details (T2) — in particular: α multiplies the runtime statistic per group; same α for both asymmetric bounds and across layers; fake-quant semantics; activation-inputs-not-embedding definition; WikiText-2 ≈120×2048 blocks; non-overlapping PPL with first-token masking; RES=128 fp16 sinks; K per-channel / V per-token, group 128; weights fp16; C4 = 256 fixed blocks; 1 % ΔPPL plateau threshold; 10k block-bootstrap; no seed replicates.\n\n**Open uncertainties:** bit-width dependence of α* beyond the two configs actually run (4-bit vs 2-bit KV; only 8-bit inputs); true granularity sensitivity (32/64 untested); whether non-overlapping 2048 hides KV damage relative to sliding-window (one check only); statistical power at ~120 blocks — if ΔPPL at 8-bit inputs is below the detectable floor, the input axis yields \"indistinguishable from FP16\", not \"insensitive\"; sink-vs-outlier attribution rests on 3 RES=0 points; PPL-transfer to downstream tasks; Llama-2 weight provenance/licence; measured runtime behaviour (my §8 numbers are unvalidated analytic estimates); whether the 3×3 interaction block is fine enough if the joint optimum lies far off-axis (mitigation: one extension allowed per refinement rule, pre-specified); whether group-adaptive MAD/PCTL families reach PPL that no fixed α achieves (only 4 overlay points — if they clearly win, a follow-up is warranted rather than a silent claim here).", "evidence": "REPO / ENVIRONMENT (verified by inspection; exploratory commands only, nothing executed as an experiment):\n1. `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` → only `/workspace/task.json`; `ls -la /workspace` → single 574-byte file. No quantization/clipping code, no LLAMA2 hooks, no datasets (no WikiText-2/C4), no eval scripts, no git metadata. Empty repo, as expected for the design-only track.\n2. `cat /workspace/task.json` → question text + `\"documents\": {\"track.md\": \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"}`; `\"datasets\": []`, `\"gpu_enabled\": false`.\n3. Dependency absence: `python3 -c \"import torch, transformers\"` → ModuleNotFoundError; `import numpy` → ModuleNotFoundError; `pip list | grep -iE \"torch|numpy|transformers|datasets|sentencepiece|accelerate|scikit\"` → empty; Python 3.14.4; `/usr/lib/python3/dist-packages` contains only OS/Ansible/apt tooling.\n4. No network: `urllib.request.urlopen('https://pypi.org/simple/')` → URLError \"Temporary failure in name resolution\"; `pip download numpy -d /tmp/x` → fails. No path to dependencies, Llama-2 weights or datasets in-sandbox.\n5. Hardware: `nproc` → 224; `free -g` → 2011 GB RAM, 0 swap; `df -h /tmp` → 1006 GB tmpfs; `gcc` 15.2.0; no GPU.\n6. `find / -maxdepth 4 -iname \"*neurips*\" -o -iname \"*94328*\"` → no matches; `/opt`, `/srv`, `/data`, `/mnt` do not exist → the neurips2024 setup topic docs are not available locally; researcher confirmed they add nothing about bit-width/dataset/sink policy.\n\nANALYTIC COST ARITHMETIC (python3 computations, not measurements):\n7. Llama-2-7B P=6,738,415,616 → 2P = 14.01 GFLOP/token forward; causal-attention term adds 3.8 % → 1.401e10 FLOP/token.\n8. Token→FLOP: 120×2048 blocks (245,760 tok) = 3.44 PFLOP; 64-block coarse subset = 1.84 PFLOP; C4-256 blocks (524,288 tok) = 7.35 PFLOP.\n9. Runtime by division: 3.44 PFLOP at 0.3 / 1 / 2 TFLOP/s = 3.19 / 0.96 / 0.48 h per full-WT2 run; 63 runs → 27–200 run-hours; wall ≈3–22 h at 8-way concurrency. GPU at 60 TFLOP/s = 1 min analytic floor → ×3 fake-quant overhead ≈ 3 min/run ⇒ ~3–10 GPU-h analytic (~10–21 h with realistic overhead/load); fp16 weights 13.5 GB; one 2048-token fp16 KV cache 1.1 GB.\n10. Group-count sanity for instrumentation overhead: at T=246k tokens, 32 layers, d=4096, group 128 → ~2.6e8 input quant groups and ~2.6e8 V groups (V: 1 group per token×layer since head_dim 128 = group_size), ~2.0e6 K groups (per-channel over 128-token chunks) ⇒ diagnostics must be scalars-only to stay in MBs.\n11. Run manifest arithmetic: shared controls 3 + P1 block 37 + P2 block 17 + cross-dataset/conv 6 = 63 runs (+≤3 refinement, +3 toy debug).\n12. CORRECTION to my earlier plan: WikiText-2's test split is ~220–260k tokens (≈120 non-overlapping 2048-blocks), not ~1.09M tokens / ~2,200 sequences. This shrinks the bootstrap unit count and is why paired tests, minimum-detectable-ΔPPL reporting and the pre-declared \"indistinguishable\" outcome branch were added to the analysis spec.", "assumptions": ["T0 task-fixed: LLAMA2-7B, asymmetric KV quantization, perplexity metric, clipping ratio as the manipulated variable.", "RESEARCHER-SET ASSUMPTIONS (not source facts): B_in=8 symmetric + B_KV=4 primary; B_KV=2 as second config; no 4-bit-input run; inputs = activation inputs to all quantized linear layers (q/k/v/o, gate/up/down), not embedding-only; inputs per-token x group128; K per-channel / V per-token, group128; weights fp16; RES=128 sink tokens fp16; non-overlapping 2048 primary + one sliding-window sensitivity run; WikiText-2 primary + C4 256 fixed blocks; decoupled Phase 1/2 then 3x3 interaction; MSE-optimal static oracle and identity fake-quant mandatory with identity as blocking gate; pre-registered tie-breaking (largest alpha in the indistinguishable set, interior requirement, WikiText-2/C4 agreement else Pareto range); deliver alpha*(bits, granularity) + one headline scalar + plateau width.", "MY DEFAULTS: alpha multiplies the runtime statistic (absmax symmetric / half-range asymmetric) per group with a single alpha per factor across all layers; grid {1.00,0.99,0.98,0.96,0.94,0.92,0.90,0.85,0.80}; extremes 0.70/0.60; fake-quant in fp16; PPL = exp(sum NLL / sum predicted tokens) with first token of each block masked; WikiText-2 test concatenated and cut into ~120 non-overlapping 2048 blocks; plateau threshold 1% delta-PPL; bootstrap over blocks 10k resamples; no seed replicates because fake-quant is deterministic; alpha_eq mapping for MAD/percentile families computed on a fixed 8-block calibration sample.", "Uncertainty is reported by resampling over eval blocks, not by seed replicates.", "Fake-quant numerical error is an adequate stand-in for real INT kernels for the purpose of measuring PPL effects.", "Llama-2-7B in HuggingFace format; weights and WikiText-2/C4 must be supplied offline by the executing host."], "uncertainties": ["Alpha* is bit-width dependent and we can only observe it at two KV bit-widths (4, 2) and one input bit-width (8); any claim of a bit-width-independent optimal ratio is beyond this design's reach.", "Granularity sensitivity beyond group_size=128 (32/64) is untested; a single per-channel-vs-per-token K ablation is the only granularity probe.", "Statistical power: with ~120 eval blocks, small input-axis delta-PPL may be indistinguishable from FP16, yielding a non-discrimination result rather than a measured insensitivity; exact WikiText-2 token/block count must be confirmed at data-prep time.", "Non-overlapping-2048 may understate KV-quantization damage relative to sliding-window; only one sensitivity run is budgeted to test this.", "Sink-vs-outlier attribution rests on three RES=0 points; a finer decomposition (per-head or per-layer sink masking) is not budgeted.", "The 3x3 interaction block may miss a joint optimum far off-axis; only one pre-specified extension is allowed.", "Group-adaptive MAD/percentile families may reach PPL unattainable by any fixed alpha; only four overlay points probe this.", "Whether weight quantization (out of scope, fp16 assumed) would change the optimal activation clipping ratio.", "Measured wall-clock and throughput are unknown; all runtime numbers in section 8 are FLOP-division estimates pending real timing.", "Llama-2 weight availability/licence provenance on the executing host; PPL-only evaluation may not transfer to downstream task quality; fake-quant ignores latency/memory consequences of alpha."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bb0cc89941ea8df0", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-bb0cc89941ea8df0", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"answer\": \"# EXPERIMENT DESIGN \\u2014 Effect of runtime clipping ratio on LLAMA2-7B perplexity (symmetric input quantization + asymmetric KV-cache quantization)\\n\\nDesign-only artifact. **No experiment was run; no perplexity values in this document are measured.** All PPL quantities are unknowns to be filled by execution.\\n\\n## 0. Provenance of every setup choice (three tiers \\u2014 do not conflate)\\n\\n- **T0 \\u00b7 Task-fixed (requirements):** model = LLAMA2-7B; KV-cache quantization is **asymmetric**; metric = **perplexity**; manipulated variable = runtime clipping ratio.\\n- **T1 \\u00b7 Researcher-set decisions (approved 2026-10-22; these are *assumptions*, not source facts):** 8-bit symmetric input/activation quantization (primary) + 4-bit asymmetric KV; second decisive config 2-bit asymmetric KV; no 4-bit-input round; inputs = activation inputs to quantized linear layers (not embedding-only); per-token \\u00d7 group128 inputs, K per-channel / V per-token, group 128; weights fp16; RES=128 sink tokens kept fp16; non-overlapping 2048 primary with sliding-window as a 1-run sensitivity check at \\u03b1*; WikiText-2 primary + C4 256 fixed blocks; decoupled sweeps then 3\\u00d73 interaction; MSE-optimal static oracle and identity fake-quant mandatory (the latter as a **hard validity gate**); pre-registered tie-breaking approved verbatim; \\\"optimal\\\" delivered as \\u03b1\\\\*(bits, granularity) + one headline scalar for the primary config + plateau width (<1 % \\u0394PPL).\\n- **T2 \\u00b7 My defaults (overridable):** \\u03b1 parameterization details, grid values, thresholds (1 % \\u0394PPL), bootstrap settings, diagnostic definitions, run manifest, cost model.\\n- **Source status:** the only source-supported setup content available is the T0 triple. Per your confirmation, `neurips2024/94328/2:setup_{1,10,17}` adds nothing about bits, dataset or sink policy, and nothing relevant was found locally (`find / -maxdepth 4 -iname '*neurips*' -o -iname '*94328*'` \\u2192 no matches). Everything outside T0 is therefore marked as assumption throughout.\\n\\n## 1. Rationale\\n\\nRuntime clipping trades two opposing errors within one scalar knob. Shrinking the quantization interval below the observed extremum reduces the uniform step \\u0394 (variance/rounding error \\u221d \\u0394\\u00b2) but introduces truncation bias on every element outside the clipped bound. Because LLM activation and K-cache distributions are heavy-tailed (a few outlier channels dominate absmax), the *optimal* bound sits below the true extremum, and how far below depends on (i) bit-width, (b) the granularity over which the statistic is computed, and (c) whether the quantizer is symmetric (absmax, no zero point) or asymmetric (min/max zero-point, more outlier-sensitive because *both* bounds are estimated from data). Asymmetric KV quantization is expected to be the more \\u03b1-sensitive factor at \\u22644 bits, since it uses a single min/max pair per group for the whole group and K has known massive channels plus attention-sink structure. The experiment is designed to (1) resolve the shape of PPL(\\u03b1) for each factor separately, (2) attribute sensitivity to the correct factor, (3) separate \\\"clipping cuts outliers\\\" from \\\"clipping cuts attention sinks\\\", and (4) deliver the practical answer as a **plateau** rather than a point.\\n\\nExpected outcome shapes, pre-declared (see \\u00a77.5 for how each is reported): (a) monotone (\\u03b1=1 best \\u2192 no clipping is optimal, report the \\u03b1 where degradation crosses 1 %); (b) interior U-shaped optimum (report \\u03b1*); (c) flat within noise (report plateau + smallest \\u03b1 meeting the threshold).\\n\\n## 2. Quantizer formalization and the \\u03b1 axis\\n\\nAll quantization is **fake-quant** (quantize\\u2192dequantize, fp16 arithmetic), so bit-width does not change compute cost and the only moving part is numerical error. Round-to-nearest, saturating to the code range.\\n\\n**2.1 Input/activation quantizer (symmetric).** For activation matrix X \\u2208 \\u211d^{T\\u00d7d}, per token t and channel-group g of size 128:\\n`m_{t,g} = max_j |x_{t,g,j}|`, `b = \\u03b1`, `s = b\\u00b7m_{t,g} / (2^{B_in\\u22121} \\u2212 1)`, `q = clamp(round(x/s), \\u2212(2^{B_in\\u22121}\\u22121), 2^{B_in\\u22121}\\u22121)`, `x\\u0302 = q\\u00b7s`. Zero-point implicitly 0 (symmetric). Applied to the inputs of all quantized linear layers: q/k/v/o and gate/up/down projections of every block (post-activation, dynamic, computed from the live tensor at inference time).\\n\\n**2.2 KV quantizer (asymmetric).** For group G (K: a channel's values over 128 consecutive tokens; V: 128 consecutive head-dim values of one token), center `c = (min+max)/2`, half-range `r = (max\\u2212min)/2`:\\n`\\u2113 = c \\u2212 b\\u00b7r`, `u = c + b\\u00b7r`, `s = (u\\u2212\\u2113)/(2^B_KV \\u2212 1)`, `z = round(\\u2212\\u2113/s)`, `q = clamp(round(x/s)+z, 0, 2^B_KV\\u22121)`, `x\\u0302 = (q\\u2212z)\\u00b7s`. Same b for both bounds. Cache positions in the RES=128 sink window stay fp16 and are never clipped (T1).\\n\\n**2.3 Primary \\u03b1 family \\u2014 scaled-max / scaled-range (S-MAX).** b = \\u03b1 \\u2208 (0,1] directly multiplying the runtime statistic. This is the primary axis; \\u03b1=1 is exactly plain min/max\\u2013absmax quantization (no clipping).\\n\\n**2.4 Alternative families, mapped onto the same axis.**\\n- **MAD/SIGMA:** bound = median + k\\u00b71.4826\\u00b7MAD (symmetric: max over |\\u00b7|), k \\u2208 {3, 3.5, 4, 4.5, 5, \\u221e}.\\n- **PCTL:** bound = empirical q-quantile of |x| (symmetric) or of x (asymmetric), q \\u2208 {100, 99.99, 99.9, 99.5, 99.0, 95.0}.\\n- **Mapping rule:** for each captured group, compute `\\u03b1_eq = bound_S-MAX_used / absmax_or_halfrange`; the family's point is plotted at the token-count-weighted mean \\u03b1_eq measured on a fixed 8-block calibration sample, with the interquartile spread of \\u03b1_eq reported as error bars. This makes all three families comparable on one \\u03b1 axis and shows that MAD/PCTL are *group-adaptive* \\u03b1's rather than different physics. Because families are group-adaptive they will not coincide exactly with any fixed \\u03b1; they are reported as overlay points, never pooled into the fixed-\\u03b1 curve's fit.\\n\\n## 3. Configuration and grid\\n\\n**Primary config P1:** B_in = 8 (symmetric, per-token \\u00d7 group128), B_KV = 4 (asymmetric, K per-channel / V per-token, group128), weights fp16, RES=128.\\n**Second config P2:** same but **B_KV = 2** (expected maximum clipping effect; this is the config that will decide the headline conclusion).\\n\\n**Sweep grid (both factors):** \\u03b1 \\u2208 {1.00, 0.99, 0.98, 0.96, 0.94, 0.92, 0.90, 0.85, 0.80} (9 points). P2 uses a 7-point subset {1.00, 0.98, 0.96, 0.94, 0.90, 0.85, 0.80}.\\n\\n**Phase structure (attribution tree, decoupled first):**\\n- **Phase 1 \\u2014 input axis:** \\u03b1_in over the grid, \\u03b1_KV pinned at 1.00.\\n- **Phase 2 \\u2014 KV axis:** \\u03b1_KV over the grid, \\u03b1_in pinned at 1.00.\\n- **Phase 3 \\u2014 interaction block:** 3\\u00d73 cross-product of the top-3 neighbourhood values from Phases 1\\u20132 (P1 only). Tests additivity and whether the joint optimum sits off-axis.\\n- **Phase 4 \\u2014 P2 replication:** Phases 1\\u20132 on the 7-point grid at B_KV=2 + a 2\\u00d72 interaction block + the RES=0 sink control.\\n- **Refinement rule (pre-specified, the only adaptive step allowed):** if the grid argmin is interior and its two neighbours differ by < 1 bootstrap SE, add \\u00b10.01 half-steps (\\u22643 runs). No other grid changes after any results are seen.\\n\\n**Run manifest (counts).** Shared: C1 FP16 baseline; C2 \\u03b1=1/1 (= \\u03b1_in=1.00 & \\u03b1_KV=1.00 anchor); C5 identity fake-quant gate \\u2192 3. P1: Phase 1 = 8 new, Phase 2 = 8 new, Phase 3 = 9, C3 extremes (0.70, 0.60 on both factors) = 2, C4 MSE-optimal static (both-factor and input-only) = 2, C7 K per-token-vs-per-channel at \\u03b1_KV=0.95 = 1, C6 RES=0 at \\u03b1_KV \\u2208 {0.95, 0.90, 0.85} = 3, MAD/PCTL overlay points = 4 \\u2192 37. P2: anchor 1, Phase 1\\u2032 = 6, Phase 2\\u2032 = 6, 2\\u00d72 = 4, RES=0 \\u00d73 \\u2192 17. Cross-cutting: C4-dataset (C4) runs = 5 (FP16, \\u03b1=1/1, \\u03b1* for P1 and P2), sliding-window 1024-stride-2048-window at \\u03b1* (P1) = 1 \\u2192 6. **Total \\u2248 63 runs** (+\\u22643 conditional refinement, +3 free toy-model debug runs).\\n\\n## 4. Data, PPL definition, determinism\\n\\n- **Primary:** WikiText-2 test split (`wikitext-2-raw-v1`), documents concatenated with BOS separators, cut into **non-overlapping 2048-token blocks**; trailing partial block dropped. Expected \\u2248245k tokens \\u2248 **120 blocks** \\u2014 *this count must be verified at data-prep time* (see \\u00a79; it corrects my earlier plan, which wrongly assumed ~1.09M tokens / 2,200 sequences).\\n- **Secondary:** C4 validation, first 256,000 documents streamed/tokenized \\u2192 256 blocks fixed as an explicit list of block IDs, identical in every run.\\n- **PPL definition:** for block i with token sequence x\\u2081..x_S, `NLL_i = \\u03a3_{t=2..S} \\u2212log p(x_t | x_{<t})`, `n_i = S\\u22121` predicted tokens (first token of each block masked; no cross-block context). `PPL = exp( (\\u03a3_i NLL_i) / (\\u03a3_i n_i) )` \\u2014 token-weighted corpus PPL (primary). Per-block mean and geometric-mean-of-block-PPL reported as secondary summaries.\\n- **Determinism:** fp16 weights, batch size 1, single forward pass per block, no sampling, fixed eval order, `torch.use_deterministic_algorithms` where applicable; quantization is deterministic \\u2192 **no random seeds and no seed replicates**; all uncertainty is resampling uncertainty over blocks.\\n\\n## 5. Controls (full list)\\n\\n- **C1 \\u00b7 FP16 baseline** \\u2014 no quantization; reference for all \\u0394PPL; per-block NLL vector cached **once** and reused for all paired tests (no re-runs needed).\\n- **C2 \\u00b7 No-clip anchor \\u03b1=1.00/1.00** \\u2014 plain min/max & absmax; the \\\"no clipping\\\" extreme and Phase-1/2 origin.\\n- **C3 \\u00b7 Hard-clip extremes \\u03b1=0.70 and \\u03b1=0.60** (both factors) \\u2014 force the degradation branch, verify monotone behaviour and that the grid's low end is beyond the optimum.\\n- **C4 \\u00b7 MSE-optimal static clip (oracle reference)** \\u2014 offline per-group clip minimising quantization MSE on a calibration set, applied statically at inference; two runs (both-factor, input-only). Provides the runtime-vs-static gap and an upper reference for what any fixed dynamic \\u03b1 can achieve.\\n- **C5 \\u00b7 Identity fake-quant \\u2014 HARNESS VALIDITY GATE (blocking).** \\u03b1=1.0 with B=16 (and the KV path likewise) must reproduce C1 within |\\u0394PPL| \\u2264 0.005 and per-block NLL max |\\u0394| \\u2264 1e-3. **Gate condition: if C5 fails, no grid run is executed and no result is reported** \\u2014 the failure is fixed first. A second gate: a 4-bit run must degrade PPL measurably vs C1 (nonzero effect) and a no-op code path must be bit-identical to C1.\\n- **C6 \\u00b7 Sink control (T1, new).** Phase-2 \\u03b1_KV points {0.95, 0.90, 0.85} repeated with **RES=0** (sink tokens quantized like everything else). \\u0394PPL(RES=0) \\u2212 \\u0394PPL(RES=128) at matched \\u03b1 attributes the KV-axis degradation into \\\"outlier clipping\\\" vs \\\"attention-sink clipping\\\" \\u2014 without this the KV attribution at the core of the question is confounded.\\n- **C7 \\u00b7 Granularity escape hatch (T1, one run).** K per-token instead of per-channel at \\u03b1_KV=0.95, to confirm the result is not an artefact of sitting in a per-channel sensitivity pit. group_size \\u2208 {32, 64} are **not** run \\u2014 recorded in limitations (\\u00a79).\\n\\n## 6. Instrumentation (cheap diagnostics, computed inside the same pass)\\n\\nPer quantized group record scalars only (no tensors, so storage \\u2248 MBs): group size, statistic used, bound, \\u03b1\\u00b7stat, number and fraction of clipped elements, clipped L2 mass `\\u03a3(x\\u2212x\\u0302)\\u00b2_outlier / \\u03a3(x\\u2212x\\u0302)\\u00b2`, quantization NMSE `\\u03a3(x\\u2212x\\u0302)\\u00b2/\\u03a3x\\u00b2`, effective step size s. Aggregate per layer \\u00d7 type (q/k/v/o, gate/up/down; K/V) \\u2192 curves of NMSE and clipped-mass vs \\u03b1, to be plotted against \\u0394PPL. Expected signature: NMSE has its own optimum at lower \\u03b1 than PPL, and K-layer NMSE dominates V \\u2014 if PPL-optimal \\u03b1 \\u2260 NMSE-optimal \\u03b1, that is itself a reported finding about surrogate metrics.\\n\\n## 7. Analysis specification\\n\\n**7.1 Primary plots.** \\u0394PPL vs \\u03b1 (\\u0394 = PPL(config) \\u2212 PPL(C1)) and absolute PPL vs \\u03b1, one panel per factor, one panel per config (P1, P2), with 95 % bootstrap CIs; MAD/PCTL overlay points with \\u03b1_eq spread bars.\\n\\n**7.2 Uncertainty.** Nonparametric bootstrap over blocks (10,000 resamples, seed 0) of the token-weighted PPL ratio. All config-vs-baseline comparisons are **paired over blocks** (identical block set): paired bootstrap on per-block NLL differences; two-sided p from the paired permutation/bootstrap; **Holm correction across the whole grid** (all comparisons in a family per config). Reported alongside the minimum detectable \\u0394PPL at 80 % power given ~120 blocks, computed before interpretation.\\n\\n**7.3 Additive attribution model.** Fit `\\u0394PPL(\\u03b1_in, \\u03b1_KV) \\u2248 f_in(\\u03b1_in) + f_KV(\\u03b1_KV)` (monotone-smooth interpolation of the marginal curves) on Phase-1/2 data; evaluate residuals on the Phase-3 block; report the full interaction matrix (\\u03b1_in rows \\u00d7 \\u03b1_KV columns), max |residual|, R\\u00b2, and an interaction F-test. Sensitivity attribution = |\\u2202\\u0394PPL/\\u2202\\u03b1| at the plateau edge per factor, plus the fraction of total joint \\u0394PPL explained by each axis; cross-checked against per-layer NMSE (hypothesis: dominant layer group predicts the dominant axis).\\n\\n**7.4 Optimum + tie-breaking (approved verbatim, pre-registered).** \\u03b1* = argmin of CI-bounded PPL. If several \\u03b1 are statistically indistinguishable (paired per-block NLL difference CI includes 0, i.e. p > 0.05 after Holm), select the **largest \\u03b1 in the indistinguishable set** (least quantization distortion, largest headroom, better transfer expectation). \\u03b1* must be **interior** to the grid (an endpoint \\u21d2 extend the grid once, then re-decide) and must **agree between WikiText-2 and C4**; on disagreement, report a **Pareto range** rather than a scalar. Additionally report **plateau width**: the maximal interval of \\u03b1 over which \\u0394PPL stays within 1 % of \\u0394PPL(\\u03b1=1.00) *and* within 1 % of \\u0394PPL(\\u03b1*) \\u2014 this interval, not the point, is the practical \\\"balance quantization error vs accuracy\\\" answer. Deliverables: \\u03b1*(B_in, B_KV, granularity) table for {4-bit KV, 2-bit KV} \\u00d7 input axis, **one headline scalar \\u03b1\\\\* for P1 (8-bit symmetric inputs / 4-bit asymmetric KV)** plus its plateau, and the P2 scalar with its plateau.\\n\\n**7.5 Outcome branches (pre-declared).** (a) monotone \\u2192 \\\"no clipping is optimal\\\"; report the \\u03b1 where \\u0394PPL first exceeds 1 %. (b) interior optimum \\u2192 report \\u03b1* and plateau. (c) whole grid indistinguishable from C1 \\u2192 report that PPL is insensitive over [\\u03b1_low, 1] with the power bound from \\u00a77.2; the answer then is the smallest \\u03b1 with \\u0394PPL \\u2264 1 % (max robustness headroom), explicitly framed as a non-discrimination result, not as \\\"clipping is free\\\".\\n\\n**7.6 Secondary checks.** Sliding-window (window 2048, stride 1024, context-preserving) at \\u03b1* \\u2014 if its \\u0394PPL differs materially from the non-overlapping estimate, the primary convention is masking KV damage and this must be stated prominently. C4 vs WikiText-2 gap \\u2192 generalization of \\u03b1*. RES=0 vs RES=128 split (\\u00a75 C6). Runtime-vs-static-oracle gap (\\u00a75 C4).\\n\\n## 8. Feasibility (analytic estimates, **not** measurements)\\n\\n**This sandbox: zero executability.** Verified: `/workspace` holds only `task.json` (no quantization code, no clipping logic, no Llama hooks, no datasets, no eval scripts); no `numpy`/`torch`/`transformers`/`datasets`/`tokenizers`/`sentencepiece`; DNS resolution fails and `pip download` fails \\u2192 no dependency, weight (Llama-2 is licence-gated; community mirror would be a provenance caveat) or dataset acquisition. Therefore nothing in \\u00a73\\u2013\\u00a77 can run here; the design must execute on a host with those artifacts. Hardware present: 224 CPUs, ~2 TB RAM, ~1 TB tmpfs, gcc 15, **no GPU**.\\n\\n**Cost model (analytic, forward-only):** 2\\u00b7P = 14.01 GFLOP/token plus ~4 % causal-attention term. WikiText-2 eval \\u2248246k tokens \\u2248 **3.4 PFLOP**; C4-256 \\u22487.4 PFLOP; 64-block coarse subset \\u22481.8 PFLOP. fp16 weights 13.5 GB; one 2048-token KV cache in fp16 \\u22481.1 GB.\\n- **CPU:** at 1\\u20132 effective TFLOP/s (224-core fp16/bf16 GEMM, memory-bandwidth-limited), one full-WT2 run \\u2248 **0.5\\u20131 h**, pessimistic 0.3 TF/s \\u2248 3 h; \\u00d71.5\\u20133 fake-quant overhead (group absmax/min-max reductions; V has ~1 group per token\\u00b7layer \\u21d2 ~2.6e8 groups per eval, so overhead is real). 63 runs \\u2248 **30\\u2013200 run-hours serial**; at 4\\u20138 concurrent runs (16\\u201332 threads each, ~55 GB RAM each \\u2014 2 TB is ample) wall clock \\u2248 **8\\u201330 h**, worst case ~1\\u20132 days. Thread-scaling is sub-linear; expect memory-bandwidth contention above ~8 concurrent runs.\\n- **GPU (whichever arrives first):** 3.4 PFLOP at 60 TFLOP/s effective \\u2248 3 min analytic floor, \\u00d73 fake-quant overhead \\u2248 **10\\u201320 min/run** \\u2192 **~10\\u201321 GPU-hours** for the full campaign on one 80 GB card (weights + KV fit comfortably; 2 concurrent runs feasible). GPU is ~10\\u201330\\u00d7 cheaper in wall clock than the CPU fallback.\\n- **Fallbacks, in order:** (1) toy-Llama harness debug (2\\u20134 Llama-architecture layers, random init, 8 blocks) \\u2014 free, validates hook wiring, \\u03b1 plumbing, PPL math, and satisfies gate C5 before any 7B run; (2) coarse grid on a fixed 64-block WikiText-2 subset for grid narrowing, with **full-eval confirmation required for \\u03b1\\\\*, the plateau and every headline number** (subset results are never reported as the answer); (3) reduced C4 to 128 blocks if needed. If neither GPU nor a dep-provisioned CPU host exists, the deliverable stays a design + harness skeleton and **no PPL claim is made**.\\n- These are FLOP-division estimates from an analytic FLOP count, not measurements; they will be replaced by measured timings from the toy-model and first real run before the manifest is scheduled.\\n\\n## 9. Limitations and untested variability\\n\\ngroup_size \\u2208 {32, 64} untested (only 128; per \\u00a75 C7 a single granularity point is tested, so granularity dependence is characterised at one point only); no 4-bit-input config, so input-bit sensitivity is a single point (8-bit) and any claim of bit-width universality for the input axis is out of reach; weights fp16 throughout \\u2014 weight quantization is known to shift activation-clipping sensitivity and is out of scope; fake-quant does not exercise real INT kernels or the memory-bandwidth/throughput consequences of clipping (\\u03b1 has zero runtime cost effect here, so \\\"optimal for PPL\\\" \\u2260 \\\"optimal for latency/memory\\\"); PPL only, no zero-shot/downstream/long-generation (\\u03b1 tuned on PPL can be suboptimal for tasks sensitive to rare outliers); one 7B model, one seed-free deterministic protocol (no weight-sampling variance); ~120 primary blocks limits power, which is precisely why the plateau, paired tests and branch (c) are the primary reporting forms; token/block count is an expectation pending data-prep verification; \\u03b1 is fixed and factor-specific \\u2014 per-layer or adaptive-\\u03b1 schedules are excluded by the question's \\\"fixed ratio\\\" framing.\\n\\n## 10. Assumptions vs. open uncertainties\\n\\n**Assumptions:** all of T0 plus everything in T1 (researcher-set) and \\u00a72/\\u00a73/\\u00a74 details (T2) \\u2014 in particular: \\u03b1 multiplies the runtime statistic per group; same \\u03b1 for both asymmetric bounds and across layers; fake-quant semantics; activation-inputs-not-embedding definition; WikiText-2 \\u2248120\\u00d72048 blocks; non-overlapping PPL with first-token masking; RES=128 fp16 sinks; K per-channel / V per-token, group 128; weights fp16; C4 = 256 fixed blocks; 1 % \\u0394PPL plateau threshold; 10k block-bootstrap; no seed replicates.\\n\\n**Open uncertainties:** bit-width dependence of \\u03b1* beyond the two configs actually run (4-bit vs 2-bit KV; only 8-bit inputs); true granularity sensitivity (32/64 untested); whether non-overlapping 2048 hides KV damage relative to sliding-window (one check only); statistical power at ~120 blocks \\u2014 if \\u0394PPL at 8-bit inputs is below the detectable floor, the input axis yields \\\"indistinguishable from FP16\\\", not \\\"insensitive\\\"; sink-vs-outlier attribution rests on 3 RES=0 points; PPL-transfer to downstream tasks; Llama-2 weight provenance/licence; measured runtime behaviour (my \\u00a78 numbers are unvalidated analytic estimates); whether the 3\\u00d73 interaction block is fine enough if the joint optimum lies far off-axis (mitigation: one extension allowed per refinement rule, pre-specified); whether group-adaptive MAD/PCTL families reach PPL that no fixed \\u03b1 achieves (only 4 overlay points \\u2014 if they clearly win, a follow-up is warranted rather than a silent claim here).\", \"evidence\": \"REPO / ENVIRONMENT (verified by inspection; exploratory commands only, nothing executed as an experiment):\\n1. `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` \\u2192 only `/workspace/task.json`; `ls -la /workspace` \\u2192 single 574-byte file. No quantization/clipping code, no LLAMA2 hooks, no datasets (no WikiText-2/C4), no eval scripts, no git metadata. Empty repo, as expected for the design-only track.\\n2. `cat /workspace/task.json` \\u2192 question text + `\\\"documents\\\": {\\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"}`; `\\\"datasets\\\": []`, `\\\"gpu_enabled\\\": false`.\\n3. Dependency absence: `python3 -c \\\"import torch, transformers\\\"` \\u2192 ModuleNotFoundError; `import numpy` \\u2192 ModuleNotFoundError; `pip list | grep -iE \\\"torch|numpy|transformers|datasets|sentencepiece|accelerate|scikit\\\"` \\u2192 empty; Python 3.14.4; `/usr/lib/python3/dist-packages` contains only OS/Ansible/apt tooling.\\n4. No network: `urllib.request.urlopen('https://pypi.org/simple/')` \\u2192 URLError \\\"Temporary failure in name resolution\\\"; `pip download numpy -d /tmp/x` \\u2192 fails. No path to dependencies, Llama-2 weights or datasets in-sandbox.\\n5. Hardware: `nproc` \\u2192 224; `free -g` \\u2192 2011 GB RAM, 0 swap; `df -h /tmp` \\u2192 1006 GB tmpfs; `gcc` 15.2.0;